<div class="coverage-note">
<p><strong>Every living entity gets a page, not just ones with drops.</strong> The roster is every
concrete entity variant that declares the "health" server behavior -- traders, villagers, and
locusts with no drop table still show up (with an empty Drops section) rather than being silently
excluded. Non-creature utility entities with no health behavior (boats, thrown projectiles,
elevators, armor stands, ...) are correctly left out.</p>
</div>
<p>
Each entity file is variant-expanded (every concrete code a <code>variantgroups</code>/
<code>allowedVariants</code> combination can produce), then: death drops and harvestable-behavior
drops are read (checking both a flat <code>drops</code> array and a glob-keyed
<code>dropsByType</code> map -- some entities only define the latter); stats (max health, weight,
hitbox size, tags) are resolved the same glob-aware way; spawn conditions are read from
<code>server.spawnconditions</code>/<code>spawnconditionsByType</code> specifically, not any
top-level field. Where a drop resolves to a "box" item using the <code>ItemStackRandomizer</code>
mechanism (e.g. the Jonas Parts&amp;Frames Stack randomizer), the actual possible contents of that
box are shown as additional drop rows, not just the opaque container.
</p>
<p>
NPC dialogue (<code>config/dialogue/*.json</code>) is cross-referenced onto whichever entity's
<code>conversable</code> behavior points at that file, flattened to two facts: every line the NPC
side of the conversation can say, and every item its dialogue can hand over as a reward. The
underlying files are genuine branching state machines (condition/jump graphs) -- that graph
structure itself isn't rendered, only the flattened lines/rewards.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Tier</strong> is the highest <code>damageTier</code>/<code>projectileDamageTier</code> found anywhere in the entity's
server behaviors (its attack AI tasks), resolved for that exact variant -- e.g. drifters run 0 (surface) to 4
(nightmare). The game compares it with armor's protection tier. The <strong>Game logic (C#)</strong> table lists the
entity <code>class</code>, every behavior <code>code</code> and every <code>aitasks[].code</code>, linked through
<code>apps/vs-wiki/src/ingest/csharpClassMap.json</code> where vssurvivalmod implements them.
</p>
<p>
<strong>3D model.</strong> The shape file an entity's <code>client.shape</code> (or <code>shapeByType</code>) names is read
from <code>shapes/entity/**</code> and drawn as plain boxes: each element's <code>from</code>/<code>to</code> and its
rotation about <code>rotationOrigin</code> (a child is positioned relative to its parent and inherits its rotation). Textures are not in the
dedicated-server download, so every texture name gets a placeholder color instead. The model starts in its rest pose; its animations (idle, walk, run...)
are read from the shape file's keyframes and can be played from the Animation picker.
</p>
